WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.930
Vygotsky's theory of social development
argues that community and language play

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.640
a central part in learning. While Jean
Piaget concluded that children's

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.310
cognitive development happens in stages,
Vygotsky rejected his ideas and believed

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:22.110
that children develop independently of
specific stages as the result of social

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.840
interactions.
Vygotsky claimed that we are born with

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.110
four elementary mental functions:
attention, sensation, perception and

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.870
memory. It is our social and cultural
environment that allows us to use these

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:47.640
elementary skills to develop and finally
gain higher mental functions. This

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.820
development
ideally happens in the zone of proximal

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.940
development. First, there is what we can
do on our own. Then there is the zone of

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.410
proximal development, which represents
what we can do with the help of an adult,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:07.140
a friend, technology, or what Vygotsky
called the "more knowledgeable other".

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:15.180
Last, there is what's beyond our reach.
To illustrate this let us think of twins

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.740
who were raised in a community in which
boys are expected to learn and succeed

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.220
while girls are only expected to be
pretty. At the age of 10 months both have

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.290
the ability to crawl and are in the zone
of proximal development for learning how

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:37.710
to stand on their feet. The more
knowledgeable other, in this case the

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.000
father, provides the boy with
opportunities to practice in a playroom

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.310
that he is equipped with scaffolding and
other objects. The boy is encouraged to

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.070
explore the equipment and eventually he
uses it to pull himself up. A few hours

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.560
later he's cruising along the structures
and a few days later he's standing on

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.780
his feet. The girl also has the potential
to stand but does not receive any

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:10.440
support in learning the skill.
When we compare the two we see that

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.170
while the girl is still trying to get up,
the boy has moved into a new zone. He

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.760
knows how to balance while standing and
now has the potential to learn how to

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.060
walk.
Both will eventually learn how to walk

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.970
but according to Vygotsky the boy will
be more skilled. The same principles

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.540
apply to all learning and the
development of higher cognitive

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.940
functions and only those learning with
the assistance of a capable mentor can

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.480
reach the full potential of their
ability. Vygotsky, therefore, believed that

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.090
inside the Zone Of Proximal Development
learning can precede development, which

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.260
means, that a child is able to learn
skills that go beyond their natural

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:02.970
maturity. He also established an explicit
connection between speech and mental

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.800
concepts, arguing that inner speech
develops from external speech via a

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.860
gradual process of internalization. This
means that thought itself develops as a

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.300
result of conversation.
Therefore younger children who don't

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:24.630
finish this process can only think out
loud. Once the process is complete inner

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.810
speech and spoken language become
independent.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:37.760
Lev Vygotsky died of tuberculosis in
1934 at the age of 37. Despite his young

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.380
age he became one of the most
influential psychologists of the 20th

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:48.380
century. He left the following advice for
educators: by giving students practice in

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:55.040
talking with others we give them frames
for thinking on their own. What do you

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.030
think? Can a child learn anything
regardless of any developmental

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:04.700
prerequisites? And do we learn only
through social and cultural contexts? And

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.830
if so, do you think it is appropriate for
a more knowledgeable other to determine

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.150
what a child should learn next? Share
your thoughts in the comments below!

